    "topic": "Lecture 1.1: Pregnancy, Parturition and the Puerperium",
    "yield_level": "High",
    "stem": "A 31-year-old primigravida undergoes a routine 10-week transvaginal ultrasound examination that reveals a twin gestation. The sonographer identifies a single shared placenta with two distinct amniotic sacs separated by a thin inter-twin dividing membrane. The inter-twin membrane inserts into the placenta at a 90-degree angle without an intervening triangular wedge of placental tissue, forming a classic sonographic \"T-sign.\" Which of the following developmental events and cleavage timelines accounts for this specific placentation?",
