    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Clinical Scenario:** The patient *thinks* she has a yeast infection because of the discharge and discomfort, but her objective findings (pH 6.5, clue cells) prove that she actually has **Bacterial Vaginosis (BV)**. (A yeast infection would have a normal pH of $<4.5$).\n- **The Consequence:** Over-the-counter yeast infection treatments (like miconazole or clotrimazole) are **antifungal** medications. BV is caused by an overgrowth of **anaerobic bacteria**.\n  - Using an antifungal cream on a bacterial infection will be completely useless. Her symptoms will fail to improve (and may even worsen as the cream causes local irritation). She requires an antibacterial drug (Metronidazole or Clindamycin) to treat the BV.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Cure:** Antifungals don't kill bacteria.\n  - **(B) Allergic reaction:** Unlikely, just ineffective.\n  - **(D) Trichomonas resistance:** She doesn't have Trichomonas (she has clue cells, indicating BV), and antifungals don't affect protozoa anyway.\n  - **(E) Chemical peritonitis:** Creams don't travel through a closed cervix into the abdomen.",
    "pearl": "**Vaginal pH** is the most crucial test to differentiate the causes of vaginitis.\n**Candida:** pH is Normal ($<4.5$).\n**Bacterial Vaginosis & Trichomonas:** pH is Elevated ($>4.5$)."
