    "rationale": "- **Treatment of Chlamydia:** *Chlamydia trachomatis* is an obligate intracellular bacterium. Because it lacks a classic peptidoglycan cell wall (it lacks muramic acid), beta-lactam antibiotics (like penicillins and cephalosporins) are completely ineffective. \n  - It must be treated with protein synthesis inhibitors.\n  - **Current CDC Guidelines (Updated 2021):** The first-line treatment for uncomplicated Chlamydia is a **7-day course of oral Doxycycline** (100 mg twice daily). \n  - *(Note: A single 1g dose of oral Azithromycin used to be first-line, but due to rising resistance and inferior efficacy for rectal infections, Doxycycline is now preferred for non-pregnant adults).*\n  - **Pregnancy Exception:** Doxycycline is teratogenic (stains fetal teeth/bone). If the patient is pregnant, Azithromycin is the drug of choice.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Ceftriaxone:** First-line for Gonorrhea, but completely ineffective against Chlamydia.\n  - **(C) Fluconazole:** For yeast (Candida).\n  - **(D) Metronidazole:** For Trichomonas and BV.\n  - **(E) Penicillin:** First-line for Syphilis.",
    "pearl": "The first-line treatment for **Chlamydia** cervicitis/urethritis is **Doxycycline for 7 days**. (Use **Azithromycin** only if the patient is pregnant or compliance is a massive concern)."
