    "rationale": "- **Treatment of Gonorrhea:** *Neisseria gonorrhoeae* has rapidly developed resistance to almost every class of antibiotics (penicillins, tetracyclines, and fluoroquinolones are largely obsolete). \n  - The current drug of choice is a high dose (500 mg) of **Intramuscular Ceftriaxone** (a 3rd generation cephalosporin).\n- **The Co-infection Rule:** Up to 40% of patients with Gonorrhea are co-infected with *Chlamydia*. \n  - If the Chlamydia test is positive OR if the Chlamydia test is *pending/unknown*, you MUST empirically treat for both. \n  - Therefore, you give **Ceftriaxone (for the Gonorrhea) + Doxycycline (for the Chlamydia)**.\n  - *(Note: If the Chlamydia NAAT returns definitively negative, you can stop the Doxycycline and just give the Ceftriaxone).*\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) Azithromycin alone:** High rates of Gonorrhea resistance; it will fail.\n  - **(C) Ciprofloxacin:** Massive fluoroquinolone resistance in Gonorrhea; no longer recommended.\n  - **(D) Ceftriaxone alone:** Inadequate because her Chlamydia status is unknown (you must cover both).\n  - **(E) Metronidazole:** For Trichomonas/BV.",
    "pearl": "If a patient has **Gonorrhea** and their Chlamydia status is positive or unknown, you must treat for BOTH: **IM Ceftriaxone (for GC) + Oral Doxycycline (for Chlamydia)**."
