    "correct": "C",
    "rationale": "- **Managing Discordant Results (Pap -, HPV +):** \n  - This scenario means the patient has an active high-risk HPV infection (positive DNA), but the virus has not yet caused any visible cellular damage (normal Pap smear).\n- **The Management Strategy:** Because the vast majority of HPV infections (even in 35-year-olds) are cleared spontaneously by the immune system within a year or two, you do not want to jump to invasive procedures. \n  - The standard algorithm for a normal Pap with a positive HPV test is to **wait and repeat the co-testing in 12 months**. \n  - If, after 12 months, the HPV is *still* positive, it is considered a persistent infection, and she would then go to colposcopy. (Alternatively, you can reflexively test specifically for HPV 16/18; if positive, go to colposcopy. But waiting 12 months is the classic, safe approach).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Immediate Colposcopy:** Too aggressive; give her immune system a chance to clear the virus first since the cells are currently normal.\n  - **(B) LEEP & (E) Hysterectomy:** Massive overtreatment for an infection without cellular dysplasia.\n  - **(D) Return in 5 years:** Too dangerous; the virus is active and could cause cancer within 5 years. She needs closer follow-up (1 year).",
    "pearl": "If a woman $\\ge 30$ has a **Normal Pap** but is **HPV Positive**, the standard next step is to **repeat co-testing in 1 year** (to see if her body clears the infection)."
