    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** A thin, postmenopausal woman with endometrial hyperplasia must have an endogenous source of estrogen. \n- **The Culprit:** The presence of a solid ovarian mass strongly points to a functioning sex cord-stromal tumor. **Granulosa cell tumors** are composed of granulosa cells, which contain aromatase and synthesize massive amounts of **estrogen**. \n- **The Consequence:** This autonomous estrogen production in a postmenopausal woman creates a state of severe unopposed estrogen, leading directly to endometrial hyperplasia and frequently concurrent endometrial carcinoma. (They also classically secrete Inhibin).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Dysgerminoma:** A malignant germ cell tumor; typically occurs in young women/teens, secretes LDH/hCG, not estrogen.\n  - **(B) Serous cystadenocarcinoma:** The most common malignant ovarian cancer; it is epithelial, not stromal, and does not secrete hormones.\n  - **(D) Yolk sac tumor:** Highly malignant germ cell tumor in children; secretes AFP.\n  - **(E) Mature teratoma:** Benign germ cell tumor (dermoid cyst); does not secrete systemic estrogen.",
