    "rationale": "- **Histologic Diagnosis of Leiomyosarcoma:** Differentiating a benign leiomyoma from a malignant leiomyosarcoma relies on the \"Stanford criteria.\" The diagnosis of Leiomyosarcoma requires the presence of at least 2 of the following 3 features:\n  1. **Coagulative tumor cell necrosis** (abrupt transition between live and dead tumor cells, indicating out-of-control malignant growth). *This is the most critical feature.*\n  2. **Severe cytologic atypia** (pleomorphic, ugly nuclei).\n  3. **High mitotic index** (usually $\\ge 10$ mitoses per 10 High Power Fields).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Whorled pattern:** The classic feature of a *benign* leiomyoma. Sarcomas are usually disorganized.\n  - **(B) Benign glands:** Describes adenomyosis (if in muscle) or a polyp (if in cavity).\n  - **(D) Plasma cells:** Describes chronic endometritis.\n  - **(E) Psammoma bodies:** Seen in Type II (Serous) Endometrial Carcinoma, not smooth muscle tumors.",
    "pearl": "The histologic triad for diagnosing **Leiomyosarcoma** is: 1) **Tumor necrosis**, 2) **Cellular atypia**, and 3) **High mitotic rate**."
