    "rationale": "- **Clinical Presentation:** An aggressive, bulky uterine tumor in an elderly woman presenting with bleeding.\n- **Pathology:** The description of a **\"biphasic\"** tumor containing both malignant epithelial components (**carcinoma**, forming glands) AND malignant mesenchymal components (**sarcoma**, forming spindle cells/cartilage/muscle) is pathognomonic for a **Carcinosarcoma**.\n- **Alternative Name:** Also known as a **Malignant Mixed Mullerian Tumor (MMMT)**.\n- **Pathogenesis:** Current evidence suggests that this is actually a highly dedifferentiated form of endometrial carcinoma (a monoclonal epithelial tumor) that has undergone epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT), meaning the \"sarcoma\" part is actually just severely mutated carcinoma masquerading as connective tissue. It is treated like an aggressive, high-grade endometrial cancer and has a dismal prognosis.\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(A) Leiomyosarcoma:** Contains *only* malignant smooth muscle (sarcoma); it lacks the malignant glandular (carcinoma) component.\n  - **(C) Adenosarcoma:** A rare tumor with *benign* glands and a *malignant* stroma (sarcoma). MMMT has *both* malignant.\n  - **(D) Type I Carcinoma:** Purely glandular; no malignant spindle cells.\n  - **(E) Choriocarcinoma:** Malignancy of trophoblasts (pregnancy).",
    "pearl": "A **Carcinosarcoma (Malignant Mixed Mullerian Tumor / MMMT)** is a highly aggressive uterine tumor containing both malignant **epithelial (carcinoma)** and malignant **mesenchymal (sarcoma)** components."
