    "rationale": "- **The Classic Differentiation:** Distinguishing Vulvar Melanoma from Vulvar Paget Disease is highly tested because they look similar on H&E stains (both have atypical cells migrating through the epidermis).\n- **Melanoma Profile:** Melanomas arise from melanocytes, which are of **neural crest origin**.\n  - They stain **positive for S100** (and Melan-A, HMB-45).\n  - Because they are not epithelial, they are **negative for Cytokeratin**.\n  - Because they do not produce mucin, they are **negative for PAS**.\n- **Paget Disease Profile:** Arises from glandular epithelial cells (adenocarcinoma).\n  - It stains **negative for S100**.\n  - It stains **positive for Cytokeratin** (epithelial origin).\n  - It stains **positive for PAS** (mucin production).\n- **Why Other Choices Are Incorrect:**\n  - **(B) S100(-), PAS(+), CK(+):** This is the exact profile of **Extramammary Paget Disease**.\n  - **(C) & (D):** Not standard profiles for these tumors.\n  - **(E) Desmin/Vimentin:** Markers for sarcomas (mesenchymal origin), not melanoma or Paget's.",
    "pearl": "To differentiate the two clear/pale cell vulvar lesions:\n**Melanoma** = **S100 (+)**, PAS (-), Cytokeratin (-).\n**Paget's** = S100 (-), **PAS (+)**, **Cytokeratin (+)**."
